-## Decision App and read boundary
-
-`agdf_decision_action` is one additional app-only facade, not a sixth model command group.
-Its strict session/presentation/revision/UUID input admits only approve, request_change and
-reject. Request change requires a nonblank comment; comments are bounded to 8 KiB and remain
-data. Approve shares the approval capability; the other actions share recording capability.
-If both capabilities are disabled the facade is absent. Model visibility metadata, session nonce
-and `ui/initialize` do not independently prove human origin. Serving and UI owners recheck
-current binding, expiry, available capability and version eligibility before dispatch.
-
-The dedicated card uses standard MCP Apps bridge calls and the existing passive document
-renderer. Its exact document has an explicit return to the same decision context. Missing proof
-never substitutes changed current bytes. Without a proven previous applicable version the
-comparison says unavailable. A committed rejection/change guards that material even if another
-presentation is prepared; changed registered material requires a fresh decision.
-
-General `agdf_cockpit_read` sessions cannot make decisions. Decision sessions use a separate
-ephemeral identity on the existing bounded reader. Four concurrent views, idle and lifetime
-expiry, generation guards and counted failed retirement prevent stale views or delayed results
-from regaining authority. Closing a view does not approve and does not invalidate independently
-eligible text. Browser/protocol tests are not native-host evidence. The production candidate
-keeps click actions disabled while native app-only mediation is unqualified; an advertised UI
-capability alone cannot enable them. No installation or host restart occurs automatically.
-
-The decision entry retires its local view synchronously and sends one existing
-`agdf_cockpit_read` close operation for its own reader. SDK resource teardown,
-React unmount and browser `pagehide` share that retirement; overlapping signals
-await the same close instead of sending duplicate requests. Ordinary visibility
-changes, scrolling and display-mode changes do not retire a live view. Late
-tool results cannot reopen it. A host-controlled navigation should await MCP
-resource teardown before replacing the document: asynchronous bridge delivery
-after abrupt navigation is not guaranteed. Browser iframe-removal evidence
-must still be checked against the actual native host's close behavior; losing
-a sidebar tab or collapsing a card alone does not prove backend retirement.
-
-The existing owned MCP-runtime marker may carry private `decision_app_qualification`
-metadata. The provenance owner checks the exact client name/version, OS/architecture,
-server/dispatcher/SDK/resource digests, the required native observations and contained
-hashed evidence files. Each view and each action rechecks that qualification. Missing,
-changed or inaccessible evidence disables clicks. This record is cooperative qualification
-evidence, not an independently authenticated human gesture, a gate approval or a tool input.
-Synthetic records test this loader; they never qualify a real host. A separately identified,
-target-bound disposable native probe prepares actual observation without installing itself.
-
-## One effect and recoverable persistence
-
-Preserve the original UUID and exact request after a missing response. `accepted` and
-`already_applied` identify confirmed effects; `rejected`, `recovery_required` and `effect_unknown`
-remain distinct. A response separates its historical receipt from a freshly available/unavailable
-`current` observation. An absent operation-status result after uncertainty does not prove
-non-execution. Do not invent another approval or UUID to resolve an outstanding decision.
-
-Canonical receipt owners remain Approval Operations, Artefact Bindings and Source Revisions.
-The append-only Run Operations extension correlates other bounded effects; Evidence Bindings
-bind newly recorded reports to their actual source/result set. No parallel decision ledger exists.
-
-Locks are acquired Run then Backlog. The owned journal publishes durable intent, stages/publishes
-declared outputs and commits the sealed Run plus receipt as its commit point. Before that point,
-recovery restores prior owned output bytes; after it, recovery verifies the committed receipt and
-finishes backlog synchronization. Mutable proposals cannot invalidate a committed source-revision
-receipt. Unknown ownership, source interference or another Run's shared backlog operation block.
-Legacy v1 Quick and v2 source-revision journals keep their own recovery; new bounded outputs
-use v3. Process-interruption evidence is not physical power-loss or native Windows durability.
-
-Closeout checks the actual registered review/QA decisions, approvals, applicable evidence,
-source references, context and obligations rather than accepting OR prose as authority. Parent
-reconciliation and programme aggregation delegate to their existing owner. A required open
-handoff blocks; an explicitly accepted-open handoff stays disclosed and never closes its parent.
-Quick Task retains OR-lite and mandatory CR; structured modes require OR-full.
